From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 11:34:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A cut-out quad is not a sprite

Patch 0027 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The foliage work of 2026-09-30 keeps a translucent draw out of the traced scene unless it is
more than two triangles, because a glow, a spark or a shaft is a quad and a quad traced as solid
casts the shadow of a quad. The same count threw out every cut-out drawn as a single quad. A
patch of vines on a wall is two triangles, so it went to the raster after the composite, took no
light, and covered the lit wall behind it with an unlit square. The user saw it beside Navi on
2026-10-08, and every Inspect view showed the square in its own color, which is what a draw on
the wrong side of the composite looks like.

An inventory of every room in the game (the decompilation's extracted scenes, walked with this
renderer's own draw call rule) found 135 draws the count keeps out. About 75 are cut-outs, nearly
all flat on walls: vines, grates, webs, the shooting gallery's and the fishing pond's painted
backdrops. The rest are water planes, light shafts, mist and glows, which must stay out. Format
does not separate the two (both are mostly IA), and neither do the combiner or the render mode.
The texture's alpha does: a cut-out has clear texels and almost no partial ones, a fade is
partial nearly everywhere, and a water plane has no clear texel at all.

So the texture cache reads each texture's alpha once, from texture memory, when the texture is
first queued (the hasher's addressing: the console's byte order, odd rows swapped, palettes in
the upper half, RGBA32 split), and keeps the answer by hash. A two-triangle translucent draw that
reads depth and samples a cut-out texture is now solid, exactly as a tree is, and the traced alpha
test gives it its holes. Glows, shafts, water and anything not yet read stay as they were.

The default and the rungs built on it take the new rule; the ladder's historical rungs keep what
they did, and rung 35 puts the count alone back for a side by side.

diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1857,6 +1857,25 @@ namespace RT64 {
                         const uint32_t acMode = otherMode.alphaCompare();
                         const int cutoutRule = int(rtResources->rtParams.tuneExperiment.x);
                         const bool manyTriangles = (call.callDesc.triangleCount > 2);
+                        // A CUT-OUT QUAD IS NOT A SPRITE (patch 0027). The triangle count above
+                        // was the way to keep a glow's quad out of the traced scene, and it threw
+                        // out every cut-out drawn as one quad with it: a patch of vines on a wall
+                        // is two triangles, so it went to the raster after the composite, took no
+                        // light, and covered the lit wall behind it with an unlit square (the
+                        // user, 2026-10-08, beside Navi: "We had fixed vines ... Yet I seem to see
+                        // those details now", and in every Inspect view the square stayed its own
+                        // color). An inventory of every room in the game found 135 draws the count
+                        // keeps out: about 75 cut-outs, nearly all flat on walls (vines, grates,
+                        // the shooting gallery's and the fishing pond's backdrops), and the rest
+                        // water planes and fades that must stay out. The texture is what tells
+                        // them apart: a cut-out has clear texels and almost no partial ones
+                        // (texture cache, textureAlphaKind). Rung 35 puts the count alone back.
+                        bool cutoutQuad = false;
+                        if (!manyTriangles && otherMode.zCmp() && (call.callDesc.tileCount > 0) && (cutoutRule != 35)) {
+                            const DrawCallTile &callTile = drawData.callTiles[call.callDesc.tileIndex];
+                            cutoutQuad = callTile.valid && !callTile.tileCopyUsed &&
+                                         (textureAlphaKind(callTile.tmemHashOrID) == TextureAlphaKind::Cutout);
+                        }
                         bool solidDraw;
                         bool castsNothing = false;
                         switch (cutoutRule) {
@@ -1958,6 +1977,14 @@ namespace RT64 {
                             // still there for anyone who wants to see it.
                             default: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; break;
                         }
+                        // The default and the rungs built on it take the cut-out quads (patch
+                        // 0027): Off, the unrelated shader experiments 1 to 7, 25 (the rule that
+                        // won, which IS the default) and 30 to 34. The ladder's own historical
+                        // rules (8 to 24, 26 to 29) and 35 keep exactly what they did.
+                        const bool defaultFamily = (cutoutRule < 8) || (cutoutRule == 25) || ((cutoutRule >= 30) && (cutoutRule <= 34));
+                        if (defaultFamily && cutoutQuad) {
+                            solidDraw = true;
+                        }
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
                         const bool wasTranslucent = (otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU);
                         if (wasTranslucent && !solidDraw) {
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_texture_cache.cpp b/src/render/rt64_texture_cache.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_texture_cache.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_texture_cache.cpp
@@ -1311,9 +1311,112 @@ namespace RT64 {
         }
     }
 
+    // THE ALPHA OF EVERY TEXTURE SEEN, BY HASH (patch 0027). A hash names the same texels for the
+    // life of the program, so an answer never goes stale, and there are a few thousand of them
+    // at most. The lock is held only to read or write one entry.
+    static std::mutex textureAlphaKindMutex;
+    static std::unordered_map<uint64_t, TextureAlphaKind> textureAlphaKinds;
+
+    TextureAlphaKind textureAlphaKind(uint64_t hash) {
+        std::scoped_lock lock(textureAlphaKindMutex);
+        const auto it = textureAlphaKinds.find(hash);
+        return (it != textureAlphaKinds.end()) ? it->second : TextureAlphaKind::Unknown;
+    }
+
+    // Counts the texels a draw samples into clear, solid and partial, reading texture memory the
+    // way the hasher above reads it: the console's byte order, each row at line * 8 bytes, odd
+    // rows with their 32-bit halves swapped, a palette's entries eight bytes apart in the upper
+    // half (CI4 offset by the palette number), and RGBA32 split between the two halves. A format
+    // this does not read (a 16-bit index, say) answers Unknown, which leaves a draw as it was.
+    static TextureAlphaKind classifyTextureAlpha(const uint8_t *TMEM, int width, int height, uint32_t tlut, const LoadTile &loadTile) {
+        if ((width <= 0) || (height <= 0) || (width > 1024) || (height > 1024)) {
+            return TextureAlphaKind::Unknown;
+        }
+
+        const bool RGBA32 = (loadTile.siz == G_IM_SIZ_32b) && (loadTile.fmt == G_IM_FMT_RGBA);
+        const bool usesTLUT = (tlut > 0);
+        if (usesTLUT && (loadTile.siz > G_IM_SIZ_8b)) {
+            return TextureAlphaKind::Unknown;
+        }
+
+        const uint32_t tmemMask = (RGBA32 || usesTLUT) ? 2047U : 4095U;
+        const uint32_t rowBytes = uint32_t(loadTile.line) << 3;
+        const uint32_t base = uint32_t(loadTile.tmem) << 3;
+        auto byteAt = [&](uint32_t row, uint32_t offset, uint32_t half) -> uint8_t {
+            uint32_t address = (base + row * rowBytes + offset) & tmemMask;
+            if (row & 1) {
+                address ^= 4;
+            }
+
+            return TMEM[(address & tmemMask) | half];
+        };
+
+        auto paletteAlpha = [&](uint32_t index) -> uint32_t {
+            const uint32_t paletteOffset = (loadTile.siz == G_IM_SIZ_4b) ? (uint32_t(loadTile.palette) << 7) : 0U;
+            const uint32_t address = (2048U + paletteOffset + index * 8U) & 4095U;
+            const uint32_t entry = (uint32_t(TMEM[address]) << 8) | TMEM[(address + 1) & 4095U];
+            return (tlut == G_TT_IA16) ? (entry & 0xFFU) : ((entry & 1U) ? 255U : 0U);
+        };
+
+        uint32_t clear = 0, solid = 0, partial = 0;
+        for (uint32_t y = 0; y < uint32_t(height); y++) {
+            for (uint32_t x = 0; x < uint32_t(width); x++) {
+                uint32_t alpha = 0;
+                if (loadTile.siz == G_IM_SIZ_4b) {
+                    const uint8_t byte = byteAt(y, x >> 1, 0);
+                    const uint32_t nibble = (x & 1) ? (byte & 0xFU) : (byte >> 4);
+                    if (usesTLUT) { alpha = paletteAlpha(nibble); }
+                    else if (loadTile.fmt == G_IM_FMT_IA) { alpha = (nibble & 1U) ? 255U : 0U; }
+                    else if (loadTile.fmt == G_IM_FMT_I) { alpha = nibble * 17U; }
+                    else { return TextureAlphaKind::Unknown; }
+                }
+                else if (loadTile.siz == G_IM_SIZ_8b) {
+                    const uint8_t byte = byteAt(y, x, 0);
+                    if (usesTLUT) { alpha = paletteAlpha(byte); }
+                    else if (loadTile.fmt == G_IM_FMT_IA) { alpha = (byte & 0xFU) * 17U; }
+                    else if (loadTile.fmt == G_IM_FMT_I) { alpha = byte; }
+                    else { return TextureAlphaKind::Unknown; }
+                }
+                else if (loadTile.siz == G_IM_SIZ_16b) {
+                    const uint8_t low = byteAt(y, x * 2 + 1, 0);
+                    if (loadTile.fmt == G_IM_FMT_RGBA) { alpha = (low & 1U) ? 255U : 0U; }
+                    else if (loadTile.fmt == G_IM_FMT_IA) { alpha = low; }
+                    else { return TextureAlphaKind::Unknown; }
+                }
+                else if (RGBA32) {
+                    alpha = byteAt(y, x * 2 + 1, 2048U);
+                }
+                else {
+                    return TextureAlphaKind::Unknown;
+                }
+
+                if (alpha == 0) { clear++; }
+                else if (alpha >= 255) { solid++; }
+                else { partial++; }
+            }
+        }
+
+        // A tenth partial at most: a cut-out drawn with a soft rim is still a cut-out, a glow or a
+        // shaft is partial nearly everywhere (the 2026-10-08 inventory of every room in the game
+        // found the two kinds far apart).
+        const uint32_t total = clear + solid + partial;
+        if (clear == 0) {
+            return TextureAlphaKind::Opaque;
+        }
+
+        return ((partial * 10U) <= total) ? TextureAlphaKind::Cutout : TextureAlphaKind::Fade;
+    }
+
     void TextureCache::queueGPUUploadTMEM(uint64_t hash, uint64_t creationFrame, const uint8_t *bytes, int bytesCount, int width, int height, uint32_t tlut, const LoadTile &loadTile, bool decodeTMEM) {
         assert(!decodeTMEM || ((width > 0) && (height > 0)));
 
+        // Patch 0027: what this texture's alpha is, while its memory is in hand.
+        if (decodeTMEM && (bytes != nullptr) && (bytesCount >= 4096)) {
+            const TextureAlphaKind alphaKind = classifyTextureAlpha(bytes, width, height, tlut, loadTile);
+            std::scoped_lock lock(textureAlphaKindMutex);
+            textureAlphaKinds[hash] = alphaKind;
+        }
+
         TextureUpload newUpload;
         newUpload.hash = hash;
         newUpload.creationFrame = creationFrame;
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_texture_cache.h b/src/render/rt64_texture_cache.h
--- a/src/render/rt64_texture_cache.h
+++ b/src/render/rt64_texture_cache.h
@@ -40,6 +40,22 @@ namespace interop {
 };
 
 namespace RT64 {
+    // WHAT A TEXTURE'S ALPHA IS (patch 0027, OoT: Recompiled). Read once from the texture memory
+    // the first time a texture is queued, and kept by its hash for the framebuffer renderer, which
+    // has to decide whether a two-triangle translucent draw is a cut-out on a wall (traced, like
+    // the trees) or a glow, a shaft or water (left out of the traced scene). A CUT-OUT has clear
+    // texels and almost nothing between clear and solid; a FADE has partial alpha throughout;
+    // OPAQUE has no clear texel, so its transparency, if any, comes from a color, not the texture.
+    enum class TextureAlphaKind : uint8_t {
+        Unknown,
+        Opaque,
+        Cutout,
+        Fade
+    };
+
+    // Unknown until the texture's memory has been seen. Any thread.
+    TextureAlphaKind textureAlphaKind(uint64_t hash);
+
     struct TextureUpload {
         uint64_t hash;
         uint64_t creationFrame;
